  "text_sample": "Your Page Title Your Website Title Studios Journal Jobs Submit Overview Buy Carbon Studios Journal Jobs Submit Overview Buy Carbon Why Kvist built a wine bar site that scrolls sideways Ottis turns every page into a row of full screen panels. Freja Holm explains how the idea survived contact with a real restaurant. Type Feature Published 18 Sept 2026 The site in this story Ottis by Kvist Ottis is a small wine bar and kitchen in Copenhagen with an ochre wall, beech tables and a menu that changes every week. Its website moves sideways. A room you walk through Freja Holm, who founded Kvist with Anders Bak, says the idea came from the room itself. \u201cYou walk in, the counter is on your right, the tables run along the window, the kitchen is at the back. We wanted the site to feel like that walk, one panel at a time.\u201d Every page is a row of full screen panels. On a phone it scrolls normally; on a laptop your vertical scroll moves you through the panels from left to right. Keeping it useful The risk with a gimmick like this is that people cannot find the menu. Kvist solved it with very plain navigation and big uppercase labels on every panel, so you always know where you are. \u201cThe booking bu",
